course: dictionary
lesson: 8995
title: Words that sound like their meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words sound like the thing they describe. Writers call these onomatopoeia. Crash, bang and buzz are examples, and so is sizzle. Say sizzle out loud and you can hear a hiss, like food frying in hot fat.
explain: «Onomatopoeia means words that sound like their meaning, like crash, bang, buzz and sizzle.» The word sizzle helps a reader hear the scene, so a story about cooking can make the reader feel they are standing next to the pan.
explain: Sizzle has other meanings too. When someone says «a sizzling 35 degrees», it means very hot. When something is «a sizzling performance», it means exciting and full of energy. Check the sentence around the word to see which meaning is right.
explain: Be careful with headlines. «Brits SIZZLE as Britain hotter than the Sahara» uses sizzle to grab attention. That may be true for one town on one afternoon, so look for the actual temperature and the weather service's advice to find the facts.
fact: Sizzle can be a verb (it sizzles) or a noun (a sizzle).
fact: «Sizzle» means to make a hissing, spitting sound like food frying in hot fat.
fact: «Sizzling» can also mean very hot, or exciting and full of energy, in informal speech.
fact: Hot oil that sizzles can spit and burn, so keep back from the pan.
fact: «All sizzle and no steak» means all show and no real substance.
exercise: «Onomatopoeia means words that sound like their meaning, like crash, bang, buzz and sizzle.» Which word is an example of onomatopoeia? | pan | summer | exciting | *sizzle
exercise: A weather report says «a sizzling 35 degrees». What does sizzling mean here? | *Very hot | Very dark | Very wet | Very windy
exercise: Someone says the new film is «all sizzle and no steak». What does this phrase mean? | A loud noise in the kitchen | Good food for dinner | Cooking meat for too long | *All show and no real substance
test: «Brits SIZZLE as Britain hotter than the Sahara» is a headline. What should you check before you believe it? | Whether the font is big and bold | *The actual temperature and the weather service's advice | How many photos there are | Whether the writer is famous
test: Why should you keep back from hot oil that sizzles? | It makes no noise at all | It is cold and wet | It will go out | *It can spit and burn you
exam: What is the name for words that sound like their meaning, such as sizzle? | Synonyms | Context | Idioms | *Onomatopoeia
